<% content_for :title, "Integration guide · SpecGuard" %>

<%# == The public integration guide (SPGD-705)
    ================================================================================================

    WHO THIS PAGE IS FOR, and why that decides its shape.

    Its reader is either a person who has just registered a repository and minted a key, or — more
    often, and this is the case the page is built around — an AI coding agent handed nothing but
    this URL by the prompt block on that repository's page. That agent has no session, cannot sign
    in, and cannot open the server's source. So:

      * it renders for a signed-out visitor, under no authentication gate at all;
      * it is complete. A reader must never have to open `Ingest::Payload` to learn a field name,
        and must never have to guess at a constraint. Every field of the envelope and of a spec
        entry is on this page, with its type, whether it is required, and — for the conditional
        ones — the condition;
      * it documents the endpoint as it actually behaves. Where this page and the server disagree,
        THIS PAGE IS WRONG. The worked payload below is not illustrative prose: it is a fixture
        (`IntegrationGuideHelper::EXAMPLE_PAYLOAD`) that `spec/requests/integration_guide_spec.rb`
        reads back off this rendered HTML and POSTs to `/api/v1/ingest`, so the document is checked
        against the endpoint rather than against a copy of itself.

    WHY RUBY IS FIRST AND WHY IT IS NOT THE WHOLE PAGE. There is a shipped Ruby client and there is
    no client for anything else, so Ruby/RSpec is genuinely the shortest path and is presented as
    it. But nothing in the ingest path is Ruby-specific: `file_path` is validated as a non-empty
    string with no `_spec.rb` suffix and no `spec/` prefix rule anywhere, and the annotation
    protocol states outright that nothing in its schema assumes RSpec or Minitest. A pytest or jest
    reporter emitting the right JSON is accepted today. So the wire contract is published in full,
    and the worked payload is deliberately a PYTHON suite — an example whose every path ended in
    `_spec.rb` would teach by illustration the opposite of what the prose says.

    WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It promises no capability the API does not currently have. There are
    exactly two routes under `api/v1` (`get "repository"`, `post "ingest"`), and this page names
    those two and nothing else. The JSON Schema is reproduced here rather than linked at its `$id`,
    because that `$id` names a host this app is not served from and routing it is separate work.
%>

<% endpoint = integration_guide_endpoint %>

<%= render UI::PageComponent.new do %>
  <%= render UI::PageHeaderComponent.new(
        title: "Integration guide",
        subtitle: "Send your suite's runs to SpecGuard — from Ruby, or from any language that can POST JSON."
      ) %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Start here", id: "start-here") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        SpecGuard learns about your suite from <strong class="text-app-content">runs your CI posts to
        it</strong>. One process posts one run; a sharded suite posts once per shard and SpecGuard
        folds those back into a single run. Nothing is scraped, polled or cloned — if a run was not
        posted, SpecGuard cannot see it.
      </p>

      <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
            ["You need an endpoint", endpoint],
            ["You need an API key", "Mint one on your repository's page"],
            ["You need a reporter", "The Ruby gem, or ~50 lines in your own language"]
          ]) %>

      <%# The read endpoint is on this page ONLY as a connectivity check and as the machine-readable
          read-back. It is emphatically not the integration, and until this guide existed it was the
          only thing the product's own UI showed anybody — a curl against `GET /api/v1/repository`,
          presented where wiring instructions should have been. Naming both endpoints with what each
          is FOR is what stops that reading recurring. %>
      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">The two endpoints</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Endpoint", "What it is for"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs break-all text-app-content">POST <%= endpoint %>/api/v1/ingest</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              The integration. One CI run in, one <code>202</code> back. Everything on this page is
              ultimately about this request.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs break-all text-app-content">GET <%= endpoint %>/api/v1/repository</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Reading back what SpecGuard has stored, and — because it answers <code>401</code>
              without a valid key and <code>200</code> with one — a one-line check that a credential
              works. It sends SpecGuard nothing.
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :neutral, title: "Annotations are optional, and always were") do %>
        <p class="mt-1">
          A run in which <em>no</em> test is annotated is valid, is accepted, and is worth posting:
          per-test durations, suite growth, the heaviest files and directories and the slowest tests
          are all computed from runs alone. Annotations (<a href="#annotations" class="underline">below</a>)
          add declared intent on top. Adopt them a file at a time — SpecGuard reports the share of
          your suite that carries one, and never refuses a run for lacking them.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Ruby and RSpec", id: "ruby") do %>
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        The shortest path: a shipped client gem adds an RSpec formatter that collects every example
        and posts the run. It never fails your build — a delivery that is refused, times out or
        cannot connect writes one line to stderr and lets the suite's own exit code stand.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">1. Install the gem</p>
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_gemfile_snippet %><% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">2. Register the formatter</p>
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_rspec_config_snippet %><% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">…or register it in <code>.rspec</code></p>
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_dot_rspec_snippet %><% end %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          The <code>--require</code> line is not optional. RSpec resolves <code>--format</code>
          against constants that are already loaded, and it cannot guess this one's path.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">3. Point it at SpecGuard in CI</p>
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_github_actions_snippet(endpoint) %><% end %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          Store the key as a CI secret — never in the repository. SpecGuard holds only a digest and
          cannot show you an existing key again; if it is lost, regenerate it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <%# The credential is the switch, and that is worth stating rather than leaving to be
          discovered: a reader who runs the suite on their laptop and sees no run in SpecGuard is
          otherwise looking at a silent success. %>
      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :neutral, title: "With no API key set, the run is written to a file") do %>
        <p class="mt-1">
          <code>SPECGUARD_API_KEY</code> is the switch. Unset, the formatter appends the run to
          <code>log/test_results.jsonl</code> — one whole run per line, the same JSON it
          would have posted — and posts nothing. That is also the fallback when a delivery fails, so
          a run survives a network blip. <code>bundle exec specguard-ingest
          log/test_results.jsonl</code> replays those lines to the endpoint afterwards. (On the wire
          a body of 256 KiB or more is gzipped, so a large run's request is compressed even though
          the file's line is not. <code>rspec --dry-run</code> writes nothing at all — there is no
          run to record.)
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Every variable the gem reads</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Variable", "Default", "What it sets"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">SPECGUARD_ENDPOINT</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">none</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Scheme and host only — <code><%= endpoint %></code>. The <code>/api/v1/ingest</code>
              path is the platform's, not a setting. There is deliberately no default: SpecGuard is
              self-hostable, so a guessed address would mean posting your test names to whatever
              answers at it.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">SPECGUARD_API_KEY</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">none</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">The repository's key. Present means "deliver over HTTP".</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">SPECGUARD_TIMEOUT</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">10</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Seconds, applied to connect, read and write. There are no retries — a retry doubles the
              worst case for telemetry that is allowed to be lost.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">SPECGUARD_COMMIT_SHA</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">CI, then git</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Falls back to <code>GITHUB_SHA</code>, <code>CI_COMMIT_SHA</code>,
              <code>CIRCLE_SHA1</code>, <code>BUILDKITE_COMMIT</code>, <code>GIT_COMMIT</code>, then
              <code>git rev-parse HEAD</code>. Set it only if none of those is right.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">SPECGUARD_BRANCH</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">CI, then git</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Falls back to <code>GITHUB_REF_NAME</code>, <code>CI_COMMIT_REF_NAME</code>,
              <code>CIRCLE_BRANCH</code>, <code>BUILDKITE_BRANCH</code>, <code>GIT_BRANCH</code>,
              then <code>git symbolic-ref</code>. A detached checkout reports no
              branch rather than the string <code>HEAD</code>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">SPECGUARD_RUN_ID</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">CI</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              The build id every shard of one run shares — <code>GITHUB_RUN_ID</code>,
              <code>CI_PIPELINE_ID</code>, <code>CIRCLE_WORKFLOW_ID</code>,
              <code>BUILDKITE_BUILD_ID</code>, <code>BUILD_TAG</code>. Sent as
              <code>ci_run_id</code>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">SPECGUARD_SHARD_ID</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">CI</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Which slice of that build this process is — <code>TEST_ENV_NUMBER</code>,
              <code>CI_NODE_INDEX</code>, <code>CIRCLE_NODE_INDEX</code>,
              <code>BUILDKITE_PARALLEL_JOB</code>. See the note below.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">SPECGUARD_OUTPUT_PATH</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">log/test_results.jsonl</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">Where a run goes when it is not delivered.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">SPECGUARD_VALIDATE_INTENT</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">unset</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Read by <code>specguard-lint</code> rather than by the formatter. Point it at a
              <code>validate-intent</code> binary to check annotations with the canonical Go
              validator instead of the gem's vendored Ruby copy; unset (or blank, which means
              deliberately off) keeps the Ruby path. A binary that is named but missing, not a file
              or not executable is already a hard exit 2 on its own. What
              <code>--require-validator</code> adds is the case that was silent — the Ruby path
              taken because no binary was ever asked for, which otherwise passes and leaves only a
              stderr line to say so. Give the flag in CI and that becomes an exit 2, whether the
              variable never arrived (a mistyped name, a step that did not run) or arrived blank;
              asking for the binary and switching it off in the same breath is refused too.
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">If you shard with a GitHub Actions matrix</p>
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_sharded_actions_snippet %><% end %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          GitHub Actions exports no per-leg index — <code>GITHUB_JOB</code> is identical across every
          leg — so a matrix-sharded suite has to name its shards itself. Under
          <code>parallel_tests</code>, or Knapsack via <code>CI_NODE_INDEX</code>, the gem picks the
          index up on its own and you need nothing here. Knapsack <em>Pro</em> is the exception: it
          exports <code>KNAPSACK_PRO_CI_NODE_INDEX</code>, which the gem does not read, so set
          <code>SPECGUARD_SHARD_ID</code> from it yourself. Why it matters is
          <a href="#sharding" class="underline">below</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Intent annotations", id: "annotations") do %>
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        An annotation is one comment line declaring what a test is <em>for</em>. It follows the
        <strong class="text-app-content">OpenTestIntent v1</strong> protocol, which is
        vendor-neutral and language-agnostic: nothing in it assumes RSpec, Minitest or a particular
        language, and the comment marker is whatever your language already uses.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">The shape</p>
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-app-border-light bg-app-surface-raised px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content"># @intent: { entity: "Order", action: "checkout", behavior: "returns 402 payment required on expired card", layer: "request" }
it "rejects checkout on an expired card" do
  # ...
end</pre>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          A single comment line, immediately above the test or on the same line, beginning
          <code>@intent:</code> and followed by one JSON object. Keys may be unquoted, strings may
          use single quotes, whitespace is free and a trailing comma is tolerated — the three forms
          below are all the same annotation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Equivalent forms</p>
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-app-border-light bg-app-surface-raised px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content"># @intent: { "entity": "Order", "action": "checkout", "behavior": "returns 402 payment required on expired card", "layer": "request" }
# @intent: { entity: 'Order', action: 'checkout', behavior: 'returns 402 payment required on expired card', layer: 'request' }
# @intent: {entity:"Order",action:"checkout",behavior:"returns 402 payment required on expired card",layer:"request"}</pre>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">The fields</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Field", "Type", "Required", "Constraint", "What it is"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">entity</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-content">yes</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">2 characters or more</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              The subject under test, as a domain noun — <code>Order</code>, not
              <code>OrdersController</code>. Keep it stable; renaming it fragments history.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">action</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-content">yes</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">2 characters or more</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">The operation or scenario — <code>checkout</code>, <code>apply_discount</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">behavior</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-content">yes</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">15 characters or more</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              One plain sentence naming the expected outcome. The minimum length is there because
              "works" and "is valid" carry no meaning to compare against.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">layer</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-content">yes</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">
              <code>unit</code>, <code>integration</code>, <code>request</code>, <code>system</code>
            </td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              The test's level, and the axis duplication lives across. Be honest: a model spec is
              <code>unit</code>, a full-stack spec hitting a route and the database is
              <code>request</code>, a browser-driving spec is <code>system</code>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">preconditions</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">array of string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">no</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">—</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">Named setup assumptions, for humans and future tooling.</td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          No other key is allowed. The schema sets <code>additionalProperties: false</code>
          deliberately, so a typo like <code>entiity</code> is rejected rather than silently dropped.
          Be aware of where that rejection surfaces, though: <code>specguard-lint</code> and the
          ingest endpoint both name it, but the <strong class="text-app-content">formatter reports a
          malformed annotation as <code>"status": "unannotated"</code></strong> — the same thing it
          sends for a test that has no annotation at all. That is deliberate. The wire has only two
          statuses, and the formatter is bound never to fail your build, so its options are to
          downgrade the one annotation or to 400 the entire run and lose telemetry for every other
          example in it. It downgrades. The consequence for you is that a typo'd annotation is
          invisible in the app — it just looks unannotated — which is exactly why
          <a href="#lint" class="underline">the linter</a> exists and why it is worth running in CI.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">In other languages</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          The marker and the object are identical; only the host language's comment syntax changes.
        </p>
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-app-border-light bg-app-surface-raised px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content"># tests/test_checkout_service.py  (pytest)
# @intent: { entity: 'CheckoutService', action: 'checkout', behavior: 'rejects the transaction on an expired card and emits a payment_failed event', layer: 'unit', preconditions: ['card is on file', 'card is expired'] }
def test_rejects_expired_card(expired_card):
    with pytest.raises(CheckoutService.PaymentDeclined):
        CheckoutService(card=expired_card).checkout()</pre>
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-app-border-light bg-app-surface-raised px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">// spec/system/checkout_flow.spec.js  (jest / playwright)
describe("expired-card checkout flow", () => {
  // @intent: { entity: "Order", action: "checkout", behavior: "shows a payment error card and keeps the cart intact when the saved card is expired", layer: "system" }
  it("keeps the cart intact", async () => {
    await page.click("#checkout");
    await expect(page.locator(".cart")).toHaveCount(1);
  });
});</pre>
      </div>

      <%# The single most likely wrong assumption a reader can leave with, so it is stated as its
          own block rather than as a clause: SpecGuard never sees your source. The annotation is
          extracted by whatever runs the suite and arrives as the `intent` object on the wire. A
          reporter that does not extract annotations still produces perfectly good runs; it simply
          sends `"status": "unannotated"` for everything. %>
      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :neutral, title: "SpecGuard never reads your source") do %>
        <p class="mt-1">
          Nothing clones or parses your repository. Your reporter reads the annotation off the line
          above the test, or off the test's own line, and sends it as the <code>intent</code> object described in
          <a href="#wire" class="underline">the wire contract</a>; the endpoint validates that object
          against the schema above and stores it. A reporter that does no extraction at all is still
          a complete integration — it sends <code>"status": "unannotated"</code> for every example.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "The linter — before your first run", id: "lint") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <%# Placed BEFORE the wire contract on purpose. It is the only thing on this page that needs
          neither a key nor an endpoint nor a deployment, so it is the fastest route from "I have
          registered a repository" to something useful — and it is usable by a project that has not
          decided whether to adopt SpecGuard at all. %>
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        <code>specguard-lint</code> ships in the same gem and needs
        <strong class="text-app-content">no endpoint and no API key</strong>. It reads
        <code>@intent:</code> annotations out of your spec files, normalizes them and validates them
        against the schema above. It is the fastest way to get value out of SpecGuard before a first
        run has ever been posted.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %>bundle exec specguard-lint --changed   # CI mode: only files in the current diff
bundle exec specguard-lint             # one-off audit: every *_spec.rb
bundle exec specguard-lint spec/models/order_spec.rb<% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">What it prints, and what it exits</p>
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-app-border-light bg-app-surface-raised px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">specguard-lint: validated in Ruby (SPECGUARD_VALIDATE_INTENT is unset)
specguard-lint: checked 12 spec files
FAIL  spec/models/order_spec.rb:9
        -&gt; &lt;root&gt;: missing required property 'entity'
specguard-lint: checked 3 @intent annotations, 1 malformed</pre>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          The count of files comes before the findings, and the count of annotations after them. The
          first line names which validator did the work and is written to <strong
          class="text-app-content">stderr</strong>, not stdout — so redirect stderr if you are
          capturing output for a report. It is printed once the validator has been resolved, which
          means a run that dies before that point does not emit it: <code>--version</code> and
          <code>--help</code>, an unrecognised flag, and a <code>SPECGUARD_VALIDATE_INTENT</code>
          naming a binary that cannot be used all produce their message and nothing else. Treat the
          line as "this is what validated the run", not as a header every invocation prints.
        </p>
        <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
              ["Exit 0", "Every annotation found is valid — or there were none"],
              ["Exit 1", "At least one annotation is malformed — the only code that is a verdict about your annotations"],
              ["Exit 2", "The linter could not do its job, and reached no verdict at all"]
            ]) %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          Exit 2 is the broad one, and it is a category rather than a list: bad flags, an unloadable
          schema, <code>--changed</code> outside a git repository or combined with explicit files or
          unable to resolve its diff base, a validator binary that is missing, unusable or disagrees
          with the gem about the schema, an unmet <code>--require-validator</code>, and any
          unexpected internal error — the linter maps its own bugs here too, so that exit 1 keeps
          meaning one thing. The rule to wire CI against is the distinction, not the enumeration:
          <strong class="text-app-content">1 is a verdict about your annotations, 2 is the tool
          reporting it never reached one.</strong> Never treat a 2 as a pass, and never read it as a
          finding — the message on stderr says which case it was.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          A <em>missing</em> annotation is never a failure. Adoption is opt-in and gradual, so the
          linter only ever objects to an annotation that is there and wrong. <code>--json</code>
          emits the same findings as one machine-readable document, which is what an agent should
          use.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "The ingest wire contract", id: "wire") do %>
    <div class="space-y-6">
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        Everything below is what the endpoint actually accepts. If your language has no SpecGuard
        client, this section is the whole integration: collect one entry per test, assemble the
        envelope, POST it once.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">The request</p>
        <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
              ["Method and URL", "POST #{endpoint}/api/v1/ingest"],
              ["Authorization", "Bearer &lt;your API key&gt;".html_safe],
              ["Content-Type", "application/json"],
              ["Content-Encoding", "gzip (optional)"]
            ]) %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          The body must be a JSON <em>object</em>. An array, a bare string or a number is refused
          with <code>the request body must be a JSON object</code>, and a body that will not parse
          at all is refused with <code>The request body could not be parsed as JSON.</code> Unknown
          top-level keys are ignored rather than rejected — with one exception: a key literally named
          <code>_json</code> is refused, because that is the name Rails gives a non-object body it
          has wrapped, and the endpoint cannot tell the two apart.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">The envelope</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Field", "Type", "Required", "Rule"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">commit_sha</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-content">yes</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Must be a non-empty string once whitespace is stripped. Required: without it the whole
              POST comes back <code>400</code> and every example in it is discarded. (<code>specs</code>
              is required too, and an ill-typed <code>branch</code>, <code>ci_run_id</code>,
              <code>shard_id</code> or <code>duration_seconds</code> refuses the run just as firmly —
              validation is all-or-nothing, never partial.) It is free-form — SpecGuard does not
              check it against your git history.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">branch</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string or null</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">no</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              A string when present. Omit it or send <code>null</code> when there is no branch — a
              detached checkout has none, and that is stored and rendered as "not reported" rather
              than guessed at.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">ci_run_id</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string or null</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">no</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Your CI provider's id for the <em>build</em>, shared by every shard of it. Must be a
              <strong class="text-app-content">string</strong> — a JSON number is refused rather
              than coerced, because <code>12345</code> and <code>"12345"</code> keying the same build
              would split one run in two. Omit it and this run is its own run.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">shard_id</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string or null</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">no</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Which slice of that build this request is. A string, for the same reason —
              <code>0</code> and <code>"0"</code> must not name different shards. Only has to be
              unique within one run.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">duration_seconds</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">number or null</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">no</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">The whole run's wall clock. Must not be negative.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">specs</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">array</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-content">yes</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              One object per example — every example, annotated or not. An empty array is accepted.
              Fields below.
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          There is no <code>total_specs_count</code> to send. The run's totals are
          <em>derived</em> from <code>specs</code>; a count supplied by the client is ignored,
          because it is the denominator of the headline metric and nobody could check it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Each entry in <code>specs</code></p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Field", "Type", "Required", "Rule"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">file_path</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-content">yes</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Where the test is <em>defined</em>, relative to your project root. Non-empty, and
              nothing more: there is no <code>_spec.rb</code> suffix rule and no <code>spec/</code>
              prefix rule anywhere in the server.
              <code>tests/test_checkout.py</code> and <code>src/cart.test.ts</code> are as valid as
              <code>spec/models/order_spec.rb</code>. Directory rollups are computed by splitting
              this string, so use forward slashes.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">line_number</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">integer</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-content">yes</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Must be a positive integer. Together with <code>file_path</code> this is the
              definition site, and the line an annotation is read from.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">status</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-content">yes</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Exactly <code>"annotated"</code> or <code>"unannotated"</code>. Send it on
              <em>every</em> entry: a missing one is not a gap, it is one validation error per
              example and a <code>400</code> for the whole run.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">intent</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">object or null</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-warning">conditional</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              <strong class="text-app-content">Required when <code>status</code> is
              <code>"annotated"</code></strong>, and validated against the OpenTestIntent schema
              <a href="#annotations" class="underline">above</a>.
              <strong class="text-app-content">Must be absent or <code>null</code> when
              <code>status</code> is <code>"unannotated"</code></strong> — an unannotated entry
              carrying an intent is refused rather than silently dropped, because dropping it would
              lose an annotation its author believed they had shipped.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">name</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-warning">conditional</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              The example's full human description — in RSpec terms, the composed
              describe/context/it. <strong class="text-app-content">Required when the entry carries
              no <code>intent</code></strong>; when present it must be a non-empty string, whether or
              not there is also an intent. Send it always. It is the only text describing a test in a
              suite that annotates nothing, and an entry with neither can be counted and nothing else.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">duration</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">number or null</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">no</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Seconds, for this one example. Must not be negative. <code>null</code> is a faithful
              record for an example that never ran; it is not the same as <code>0</code>, which
              enters the slowest-test and per-file aggregates as a real measurement.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">id</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">no, but send it</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              This example's identity <em>within this run</em>, unique across the whole run. It is
              the key a re-delivery is reconciled on, so it is what makes re-running a shard replace
              its previous numbers instead of doubling them. Unique per example rather than per
              coordinate — a table-driven loop puts N examples on one line, and a shared helper puts
              several files' examples on one coordinate, so <code>file_path:line_number</code> is the
              coordinate of the <em>code</em> and cannot key a measurement.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">spec_file_path</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">no, but send it</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              The file that <em>ran</em> this example, which differs from <code>file_path</code> only
              when the test is defined in a shared helper. This is what per-file and per-directory
              time is aggregated by, so sending it is the difference between "this suite file costs
              4s" and every shared example's cost being attributed to the helper. Omitted, it falls
              back to <code>file_path</code>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">outcome</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-muted">no, but send it</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              What happened — the Ruby client sends <code>passed</code>, <code>failed</code> or
              <code>pending</code>. Free text, echoed back verbatim and never interpreted, so any
              vocabulary your runner uses is accepted. Use one consistently.
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>

        <%# The three "no, but send it" rows are the ones a reader could not have discovered from
            the endpoint's own error messages — nothing is ever refused for their absence, so a
            reporter built by probing the API would omit all three and quietly lose per-example
            identity, per-file attribution and outcomes. Naming them as a group is the point; a
            reader who skims the table still meets them here.

            The TITLE says what is still true of all three — nothing refuses you for omitting them —
            rather than the older "you are never told you are missing", which this endpoint's
            `identified_specs` counter made false for `id` and which would otherwise have shipped
            as a documented lie. Two of the three are still undiscoverable by probing; `id` is not,
            and the second paragraph draws exactly that line rather than blurring the group. %>
        <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, title: "Three fields nothing will refuse you for omitting") do %>
          <p class="mt-1">
            <code>id</code>, <code>spec_file_path</code> and <code>outcome</code> are not validated.
            A payload omitting all three is accepted with a <code>202</code> and looks completely
            healthy — and quietly gives up per-example identity across re-deliveries, correct
            per-file time attribution, and pass/fail history. Send all three.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2">
            <code>spec_file_path</code> and <code>outcome</code> are the two you
            <strong class="text-app-content">cannot</strong> learn about by probing the endpoint,
            which is why they are called out here rather than left in the table. <code>id</code> is
            the exception: the <code>202</code> reports <code>identified_specs</code> beside
            <code>recorded_specs</code>, so a producer omitting ids can see the shortfall in the
            response instead of being told nothing. Compare those two — never
            <code>total_specs</code>, which counts something else (see “What comes back”).
          </p>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <%# The fixture. `id` on the WRAPPER rather than on the <code> element, because the Copy
          button is a sibling inside it and the verification spec wants the block, not the button. %>
      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text" id="<%= IntegrationGuideHelper::EXAMPLE_PAYLOAD_ELEMENT_ID %>">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">A complete, accepted request body</p>
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_example_payload %><% end %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          One annotated example and one unannotated one, from a Python suite — because nothing here
          is Ruby-specific. This exact document is posted to the live endpoint by SpecGuard's own
          test suite, so it is the contract rather than an illustration of it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Posting it</p>
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_curl_snippet(endpoint) %><% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">What comes back</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Status", "Body", "Meaning"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">202</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content-secondary break-all">{"test_run_id":…,"total_specs":2,"annotated_specs":1,"annotated_ratio":0.5,"recorded_specs":2,"identified_specs":2,"embedding_status":"queued"}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Stored. <code>total_specs</code>, <code>annotated_specs</code> and
              <code>annotated_ratio</code> are the whole run's — every shard so far, not just this
              request's slice. <code>annotated_ratio</code> is a 0–1 fraction, or
              <code>null</code> when the run has no examples at all: a run of nothing has no share
              to report, and <code>0.0</code> there would be indistinguishable from a measured zero
              share of a suite that does have tests. The null follows the run's total, not your
              payload — posting <code>specs: []</code> is accepted, and returns <code>null</code>
              only when it leaves the run still empty. Send an empty shard to a run whose other
              shards reported examples and you get their number back. Parse the field as nullable
              either way. <code>recorded_specs</code> and <code>identified_specs</code> are a
              <strong class="text-app-content">different grain</strong>: they count the per-example
              <em>rows</em> the run stored, and how many of those carried an <code>id</code>. Both
              are always integers — <code>0</code>, never <code>null</code>, since “no row carried
              an id” is something measured rather than undefined. Your id shortfall is
              <code>recorded_specs − identified_specs</code>; anything above zero means part of the
              run has no upsert key. What that costs depends on how you deliver: post slices with a
              <code>ci_run_id</code> but no <code>shard_id</code> and re-delivering one appends
              duplicate rows instead of replacing them
              (<a href="#sharding" class="underline">below</a>). Named shards replace themselves
              either way — the delete does that, not the ids. What the ids buy you there is
              insurance: drop the <code>shard_id</code> and those slices become the case above,
              where the ids are the only thing keeping a re-delivery from duplicating rows. They
              are not what carries a test from one run to the next: <code>id</code> is RSpec's positional
              <code>Example#id</code>, unique within a run only, and SpecGuard follows a test across
              runs by its <em>text</em> — its name and intent — not by its id. <strong class="text-app-content">Do not subtract from
              <code>total_specs</code></strong> — that is the run's reported suite size across all
              shards, and it counts what you <em>sent</em>, while these count what was
              <em>stored</em>. The two legitimately differ: repeat an <code>id</code> within one
              payload and it stores one row, so a producer sending every id it has would compute a
              shortfall that does not exist. <code>embedding_status</code> is
              <code>"queued"</code> when identity resolution was
              scheduled and <code>"pending"</code> when there was nothing to schedule.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">400</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content-secondary break-all">{"error":"bad_request","message":"…","details":["…"]}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Refused, and nothing was stored. <code>details</code> carries
              <strong class="text-app-content">every</strong> failure found, not just the first —
              <code>message</code> repeats the first one. A single bad example can produce several
              entries, because each field is checked independently. Entries about an example are
              prefixed <code>specs[i]</code> and then named by whichever coordinates the entry
              actually has: <code>specs[0] spec/order_spec.rb:9:</code> with both,
              <code>specs[0] spec/order_spec.rb:</code> with only a path,
              <code>specs[0] 9:</code> — a bare line number, not a path — with only a line, and
              <code>specs[0]:</code> with neither. The partial forms are not curiosities; omitting
              <code>line_number</code> is the mistake a new reporter makes first. Entries about the
              envelope name the offending field instead. The exceptions are the two refusals that
              happen before any field is looked at: a body that is not JSON at all
              (<code>The request body could not be parsed as JSON.</code>) and a body that parses
              to something other than an object (<code>the request body must be a JSON object</code>).
              Each is refused with that one entry alone, naming no field, because nothing further
              was read. Refusals are also listed on your repository's page, so a
              pipeline that is 400ing is visible in the app and not only in your CI log.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">401</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content-secondary break-all">{"error":"unauthorized","message":"A valid Bearer API key is required."}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              The key was missing, malformed, revoked or rotated. No detail about which, and nothing
              is recorded against the repository — there is no repository to record it against.
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Compression</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
          Send <code>Content-Encoding: gzip</code> and gzip the body; the endpoint inflates it and
          reads it as ordinary JSON. <code>Content-Type</code> still describes the body
          <em>inside</em> the encoding, so it stays <code>application/json</code>. This is worth
          doing for a large suite — a 20,000-example run is around 7 MiB identity-encoded and about
          0.33 MiB gzipped, which is the difference between a run that cannot be written inside a
          10-second timeout on a slow link and one that can. The inflated body is capped at 64 MiB,
          and only the first gzip member is read, so concatenated streams silently lose everything
          after the first.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3" id="sharding">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Sharded suites</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
          Nobody runs a 20,000-example suite in one process. Each shard posts its own slice, and
          SpecGuard folds them into one run keyed on <code>ci_run_id</code>. The run's totals are
          re-derived from the slices after every delivery rather than accumulated, which is what
          makes the whole thing idempotent — but only if you name the parts:
        </p>
        <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
              ["Same ci_run_id, distinct shard_id", "One run. A shard that reports twice replaces itself."],
              ["Same ci_run_id, no shard_id", "One run, but a re-delivered slice is counted twice."],
              ["No ci_run_id", "Every POST is its own run."]
            ]) %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          Provider run ids deliberately survive a re-run — GitHub's <code>GITHUB_RUN_ID</code> does
          not change when you re-run a workflow — which is what lets "re-run failed jobs" land a
          retried shard back on the run it belongs to instead of forming a new, partial one. Pair it
          with a <code>shard_id</code> and durations behave too: the run's wall clock is the
          <em>maximum</em> across shards, not the sum, because four eight-minute shards took eight
          minutes and not thirty-two.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Writing a reporter for another language", id: "other-languages") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        There is no packaged client for pytest, jest, Go's <code>testing</code> or anything else
        today. There does not need to be: the contract above is the whole interface, and a reporter
        is a hook that collects a few fields per test and one HTTP POST at the end. Everything
        SpecGuard shows — per-test duration, suite growth, the heaviest files and directories,
        repeated descriptions — is computed from exactly those fields and is not Ruby-aware.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">The checklist</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
          <li>
            Hook whatever your framework calls "a test finished" — pytest's
            <code>pytest_runtest_logreport</code>, jest's custom reporter
            <code>onTestCaseResult</code>, Go's <code>-json</code> output.
          </li>
          <li>
            Per test, record: the defining file and line, a run-unique <code>id</code>, the file that
            ran it, the full description as <code>name</code>, the duration in seconds, and the
            outcome.
          </li>
          <li>
            Optionally read the <code>@intent:</code> comment on the line above the test, or
            trailing on the test's own line — both are legal. If you
            find one, parse it and send <code>"status": "annotated"</code> with the object; if not,
            send <code>"status": "unannotated"</code> and no intent. Start without this — an
            unannotated suite is a complete, useful integration.
          </li>
          <li>
            At the end of the process, assemble the envelope and POST it once, with the key as a
            Bearer token. One process, one request.
          </li>
          <li>
            If your suite is sharded, set <code>ci_run_id</code> from your CI provider's build id and
            <code>shard_id</code> from the slice index. Post once per shard.
          </li>
          <li>
            Never fail the build over telemetry. Treat every error, timeout and non-2xx as "log one
            line and move on" — and consider writing the body to a file so a failed delivery can be
            replayed rather than lost.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :neutral, title: "If a run is refused, the endpoint tells you exactly why") do %>
        <p class="mt-1">
          A <code>400</code> lists every problem it found in <code>details</code>. Entries about an
          example are prefixed with its index in <code>specs</code> and then named by whatever
          coordinates that entry has — <code>specs[3] spec/models/order_spec.rb:9: …</code> with
          both, and, when one is missing, the other alone: <code>specs[3] spec/models/order_spec.rb:
          …</code> or a bare <code>specs[3] 9: …</code>. An entry malformed enough to have neither
          falls back to <code>specs[3]</code> on its own. Dropping <code>line_number</code> is the
          case a new reporter hits first, so expect the path-only form early. Entries about the
          envelope name the offending field instead. One bad example can yield several entries.
          Build against that rather than against guesswork: post a two-example run by hand with
          <code>curl</code> first, read what comes back, and only then wire the reporter in.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "The MCP bridge, for coding agents", id: "mcp") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <%# Described honestly as bootstrap-stage. It is real and it works, and it installs from a
          source checkout because it is not published to a package registry — so anything here that
          implied `npx specguard-mcp` would be a promise the project cannot keep. %>
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        <code>specguard-mcp</code> is a small MCP server that gives an AI coding agent direct access
        to two things: your repository's SpecGuard overview, and the annotation linter. It reuses
        <code>SPECGUARD_ENDPOINT</code> and <code>SPECGUARD_API_KEY</code> — the same two variables
        the gem uses — so a repository that is already reporting runs is already configured for it.
      </p>

      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, title: "Bootstrap-stage: install from a checkout") do %>
        <p class="mt-1">
          It is not on npm. You clone it and build it yourself, and you point your MCP client at the
          built file by absolute path. That is a deliberate statement of where it is, not a step
          that is about to get shorter.
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Build it</p>
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_mcp_install_snippet %><% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Register it with your agent</p>
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_mcp_config_snippet(endpoint) %><% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">What it exposes</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Tool", "What it does"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">lint_intent_annotations</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Runs your project's own <code>specguard-lint</code> and returns the findings. Needs no
              endpoint and no key.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">get_repository_overview</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              The repository's latest run, heaviest files and directories, slowest examples, repeated
              descriptions and run history — the dashboard, without a browser. Needs both variables.
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
